[
    {
        "content": "<p>Hello, I'm another member of the University of Nebraska-Lincoln team that posted the topic General//general//Formalizing Max Flow/Min Cut, and I wanted to ask about whether anyone has worked on <a href=\"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3527095\">Q3527095:</a> Jacobi's four-square theorem, which is on the 1000 Missing Theorems list on the MathLib website. I have made progress on a Lean implementation already. (I didn't know about the proper procedures until recently, and even then I'm still confused. Apologies if someone has been working on it already.)</p>\n<p>I found a proof online that I'm following in my write-up, but it requires the Jacobi Triple Product formula. I found <a href=\"https://faabian.github.io/algebraic-combinatorics/targets.html\">a package that proves the Jacobi Triple Product</a> in <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Q</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⟦</mo><mi>x</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⟧</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{Q}\\llbracket x\\rrbracket</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Q</span><span class=\"mopen\"><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:-0.1778em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\"><span class=\"mclose\">]</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:-0.1778em;\"></span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span></span> (the last two theorems in <a href=\"https://faabian.github.io/algebraic-combinatorics/docs/AlgebraicCombinatorics/PentagonalJacobi.html#AlgebraicCombinatorics.jacobiRHSEval\">this page of its documentation</a>), but I need the version that applies in <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span></span></span></span> (<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacobi_triple_product\">Wikipedia page for Jacobi Triple Product</a>). Has anyone worked on the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span></span></span></span> version of the Jacobi Triple Product?</p>",
        "id": 606574174,
        "sender_full_name": "Danny",
        "timestamp": 1782415932
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Can you not map the Q-version up to C with <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=PowerSeries.map%3F#doc\">docs#PowerSeries.map?</a></p>",
        "id": 606576302,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1782416688
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't think so: I need the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span></span></span></span> version, not the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⟦</mo><mi>x</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⟧</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{C}\\llbracket x\\rrbracket</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span><span class=\"mopen\"><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:-0.1778em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\"><span class=\"mclose\">]</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:-0.1778em;\"></span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span></span> version</p>",
        "id": 606576992,
        "sender_full_name": "Danny",
        "timestamp": 1782416926
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Another way to prove Jacobi's formula is via quaternion algebra. Denote <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_4(n)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0278em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">4</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> the number of ways to represent <code>n</code> as sums of 4 squares. Then it is just the number of elements in the ring <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{Z}[1,i,j,k]</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Z</span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0572em;\">j</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span> with reduced norm <code>n</code>. This ring is of index 2 in a maximal order <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">O</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{O}=\\mathbb{Z}[1,i,j,(1+i+j+k)/2]</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">O</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Z</span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0572em;\">j</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7429em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">i</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.854em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0572em;\">j</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mord\">/2</span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span>, so <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_4(n)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0278em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">4</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> is closely related to the number of elements in this ring of reduced norm <code>n</code> (depending on the parity of <code>n</code>; need to analyze the units group of these two rings; leave as exercise). One proves that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">O</span></span></span></span> is an Euclidean domain, so PID. Hence one eventually deduces that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_4(n)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0278em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">4</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> is essentially multiplicative. For an odd prime number <code>p</code>, the number of (principal) right ideals of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">O</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">O</span></span></span></span> with reduced norm <code>p</code> (closely related to <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_4(p)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0278em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">4</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>) can be counted via local-global principle: its localization at primes <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q\\neq p</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">q</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mord vbox\"><span class=\"thinbox\"><span class=\"rlap\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"inner\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mrel\"></span></span></span><span class=\"fix\"></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace nobreak\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span></span></span></span> must be <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">O</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{O}_q</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.9694em;vertical-align:-0.2861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">O</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0278em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">q</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2861em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> (only one choice) while its localization at <code>p</code> is a (principal) right ideal of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">O</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{O}_p</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.9694em;vertical-align:-0.2861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">O</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0278em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">p</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2861em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> of reduced norm <code>p</code> (several choices), whose number can be easily counted, because <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">O</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal{O}_p\\cong M_2(\\mathbb{Z}_p)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.9694em;vertical-align:-0.2861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathcal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">O</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0278em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">p</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2861em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≅</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.0361em;vertical-align:-0.2861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">M</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.109em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Z</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">p</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2861em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> is the matrix algebra.</p>",
        "id": 606654780,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1782459074
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If we had Weyl-Kac denominator formulas of Kac-Moody Lie algebras, Jacobi would be the affine sl2 case.<br>\nAre you sure you need complex substitutions?</p>",
        "id": 606672135,
        "sender_full_name": "Scott Carnahan",
        "timestamp": 1782464596
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This is the paper I'm following: <a href=\"https://www.jstor.org/stable/2589321?seq=2\">https://www.jstor.org/stable/2589321?seq=2</a></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Another way to prove Jacobi's formula is via quaternion algebra</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Just making sure, do you mean Jacobi's Triple Product? If so, I don't follow: Where does <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span></span></span></span> or <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⟦</mo><mi>x</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⟧</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{C}\\llbracket x\\rrbracket</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span><span class=\"mopen\"><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:-0.1778em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\"><span class=\"mclose\">]</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:-0.1778em;\"></span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span></span> come into play? If not, this may be using tools that I'm not familiar with, which makes me concerned about whether it would be a good project for me. I also want to follow my current paper.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Are you sure you need complex substitutions?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I think so (unless I use a different paper or learn some new techniques). If you use <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Q</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⟦</mo><mi>x</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⟧</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{Q}\\llbracket x\\rrbracket</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Q</span><span class=\"mopen\"><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:-0.1778em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\"><span class=\"mclose\">]</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:-0.1778em;\"></span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span></span> or <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⟦</mo><mi>x</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⟧</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{C}\\llbracket x\\rrbracket</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span><span class=\"mopen\"><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:-0.1778em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\"><span class=\"mclose\">]</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:-0.1778em;\"></span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span></span>, the paper I'm following would require you to divide by <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-q^n x + q^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8588em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">q</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.0085em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">q</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> in Equation 1, which seems to require taking power series in the Laurent variable. Further, the paper would then require some sort of limit of those expressions to get Equation 2. I could see some measure theory coming into play for the second issue (I was suggested to use the Dominated Convergence Theorem), but I'm not very familiar with measure theory in Lean. I wanted to avoid having to learn how to apply measure theory to polynomials in Lean to avoid having too large of a scope for my first Lean project.</p>",
        "id": 606773323,
        "sender_full_name": "Danny",
        "timestamp": 1782494198
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1098782\">Danny</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Formalizing.20Four.20Squares.20Theorem/near/606773323\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Just making sure, do you mean Jacobi's Triple Product?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>No, I mean Jacobi's formula on the number of ways to represent a natural number to sums of 4 squares. Which is  your Wikipedia link <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacobi%27s_four-square_theorem\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacobi%27s_four-square_theorem</a>. I mean there is a proof without any q-expansion or modular forms.</p>",
        "id": 606880650,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1782541730
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This is good to know, but I'll probably continue with my current approach. Thank you!</p>",
        "id": 607220132,
        "sender_full_name": "Danny",
        "timestamp": 1782759794
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Did you yet convince yourself that you really needed the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span></span></span></span> version? It seems to me that the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{Q}[[x]]</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Q</span><span class=\"mopen\">[[</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">]]</span></span></span></span> version might be strictly stronger, because it proves an identity without any assumptions about convergence. However one loses the ability to apply theorems from complex analysis -- but does one really need any?</p>",
        "id": 607222478,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1782760675
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"110038\">@Kevin Buzzard</span> I think I do need the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span></span></span></span> version. My above response to \"Are you sure you need complex substitutions?\" explains my current reasoning, but these are my main reasons:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>I'll need to divide by polynomials in <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span></span></span></span> (not necessarily monomials)</li>\n<li>I wanted to avoid going into Measure Theory for my current project since I'm not familiar enough with Lean's Measure Theory implementation to confidently apply the Dominated Convergence Theorem for the measurable space <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Q</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⟦</mo><mi>x</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⟧</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{Q}\\llbracket x\\rrbracket</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Q</span><span class=\"mopen\"><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:-0.1778em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\"><span class=\"mclose\">]</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:-0.1778em;\"></span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span></span>. <ul>\n<li>\"What is the measure for <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Q</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⟦</mo><mi>x</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">⟧</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{Q}\\llbracket x\\rrbracket</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Q</span><span class=\"mopen\"><span class=\"mopen\">[</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:-0.1778em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">[</span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\"><span class=\"mclose\">]</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:-0.1778em;\"></span><span class=\"mclose\">]</span></span></span></span></span>?\" is one question I would need to answer, and my Measure Theory class didn't address this measurable space. I would need to check that the Lean implementation doesn't make it impossible to extract what I need after applying the proper theorem</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>",
        "id": 607704407,
        "sender_full_name": "Danny",
        "timestamp": 1782939866
    }
]